From: qwen36-fast iter-17
Subject: [PATCH] attempt to unlock K>1 in MTP speculative decoding

This patch explores what it would take to make `--draft-max N` with
`--spec-type mtp` actually produce N draft tokens per step rather than 1.

**Finding: There is no `n_max = 1` hardcode to remove.**

The iter-15 claim of a cap at `tools/server/server.cpp:1309` was inaccurate.
That line actually reads:

    backend_sampling &= !(slot.can_speculate() && task.params.speculative.n_max > 0);

— a boolean gate on backend sampling, not a value clamp.

The real constraint is architectural, in `common/speculative.cpp`:
`common_speculative_state_mtp::draft()` (lines 603-649) is hard-coded to
produce **at most 1 draft token per call**:

  1. It reads `llama_get_mtp_logits(ctx_tgt)` — a SINGLE vocab-sized vector
     of logits that the main-model decode stashed on the GPU.
  2. It argmaxes those logits → one token.
  3. `result.push_back(draft_token)` — pushes exactly one.
  4. Returns.

`params.n_max` is not even read in the function body (`GGML_UNUSED(params)`).
Upping `--draft-max` can only ever be a no-op for MTP on PR #20700.

**Why this is not a 20-30 LoC fix:**

To produce K>1 tokens, we'd need one of:

(a) Chained single-token MTP calls on the DRAFT context.
    Each additional token would require a fresh micro-decode that runs ONLY
    the MTP head on a 1-token batch seeded with the just-drafted token.
    No such API exists in llama.h. `llama_decode` always runs the full
    backbone, which is exactly what we're trying to avoid.

(b) Re-architect `build_mtp_head` to emit `t_logits_mtp` of shape
    `[n_vocab, K]` — i.e. a K-step unrolled cascade inside one graph.
    The existing code at qwen35.cpp:536-539 IS a cascade loop, but it
    iterates over DISTINCT MTP layers (DeepSeek-MTP style, where multiple
    `nextn.eh_proj` tensors stack one after another to IMPROVE a single
    token's prediction — not to produce multiple future tokens).
    Line 533: `res->t_logits_mtp = mtp_logits;` — overwrites each
    iteration; only the LAST layer's output is retained.

    Adding a genuine multi-token cascade would require:
      - K separate `mtp_logits_k` tensors in `llm_graph_result`
      - Per-K argmax → embedding → next-layer input wiring
      - Per-K buffer extraction in `llama-context.cpp:1819-1835`
      - Per-K KV slot management (each lookahead token needs its own
        attention position)
      - Per-K recurrent-state checkpoints for the Gated DeltaNet layers

    Conservative estimate: 400-600 LoC across 6+ files. Out of scope for a
    timeboxed iteration.

(c) Duplicate the single MTP layer K times in the GGUF (bump
    `nextn_predict_layers = 1 → K`). This runs the graph-level cascade
    over K identical layers, BUT:
      - Only the K-th (deepest) token's logits end up in `t_logits_mtp`
        (same issue as (b))
      - Even if we got intermediate layers' logits, they'd all predict the
        SAME position relative to the backbone — DeepSeek-MTP's design
        choice. Qwen3.6 is not trained for shift-k prediction.
      - α would collapse (the untrained deeper heads produce noise for
        a task they weren't trained for)

**Conclusion: PR #20700 MTP on Qwen3.6 is intrinsically a single-token
drafter. K>1 cannot be unlocked by patching the server or the speculative
state; it would require retraining the MTP head with shift-k targets AND
rewriting the graph cascade.**

This file is intentionally empty of actual code changes — it documents
WHY the cap is not a hardcode. The empirical evidence is in
`bench/10-mtp-cascade.md`: K=1,2,3,4 all produce identical output,
identical `draft_n=127` out of 256 predicted tokens, at ~7.6 tps (30%
regression vs Q2_K_XL baseline of 13.82 tps).

No patch is applied. Reverts cleanly (`git apply --check` will report no
hunks to apply).
